PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L09U1.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":9,"cefr":"B2b","unit":1,"theme":"Identity and Belonging","essential_question":"What shapes who we are and where we belong?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1","title":"Having Spent Five Years Abroad…: Participle Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to use present, past, perfect and perfect passive participle clauses.","Students will be able to form negative participle clauses and keep the same subject in both parts of the sentence.","I can write \"Having spent five years abroad, I had changed.\"","I can write \"Not knowing anyone, I sat alone.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول والصيغ التامة والتامة المبنية للمجهول.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تكوين جمل النفي بهذه الصيغ والإبقاء على الفاعل نفسه في جزأي الجملة.","أستطيع أن أكتب Having spent five years abroad, I had changed.","أستطيع أن أكتب Not knowing anyone, I sat alone."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"identity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈdɛntɪˌti/","definition":"who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different","arabic":"هوية","example":"Language is an important part of our identity.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W01"},{"word":"belong","pos":"verb","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋ/","definition":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or group","arabic":"ينتمي","example":"After a few months, I finally felt that I belonged.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W02"},{"word":"abroad","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/əˈbrɔd/","definition":"in or to a foreign country","arabic":"في الخارج","example":"My brother is studying abroad in Malaysia.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W03"},{"word":"unfamiliar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌʌnfəˈmɪljɚ/","definition":"not known or not experienced before","arabic":"غير مألوف","example":"Everything in the new city felt unfamiliar.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"A participle clause replaces a full clause and makes writing more concise. A present participle (-ing) shows an active action at the same time as the main verb: Arriving in January, we were shocked. (= When we arrived…) A past participle (-ed/-en) has a passive meaning: Encouraged by a teacher, I joined the club. (= Because I was encouraged…)\nA perfect participle (having + past participle) shows that the action happened before the main action: Having read the same novels, I had something to talk about. The perfect passive (having been + past participle) is for an earlier passive action: Having been raised by his grandparents, he loved old stories. Make negatives with not before the participle: Not knowing anyone, I sat alone.\nThe subject of the participle clause must be the same as the subject of the main clause: Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada. (I was born.) Otherwise the clause dangles: (not: Born in Riyadh, my family moved to Canada) (unless the whole family was born there!).\n-ing = active, same time • -ed/-en = passive\nhaving + past participle = earlier action\nhaving been + past participle = earlier passive action\nnot + participle = negative • same subject in both parts","examples":["Raised in a bilingual family, Lina speaks Arabic and English fluently.","Looking at old photos, my grandfather told us about his childhood.","Having lived in four countries, Omar adapts easily to new places.","Not understanding the joke, I smiled politely.","Having been born in Jeddah, he always feels at home by the sea.","Surrounded by unfamiliar faces, she felt nervous.","Having finished his studies, my uncle returned to Saudi Arabia.","Speaking three languages, she works as a translator."],"common_errors":["✗ Having been lived abroad, he speaks English well. → ✓ Having lived abroad, he speaks English well. (Active earlier action → having + past participle.)","✗ Knowing not the language, I felt lost. → ✓ Not knowing the language, I felt lost. (not goes before the participle.)","✗ Encouraging by her teacher, she joined the club. → ✓ Encouraged by her teacher, she joined the club. (Passive meaning → past participle.)","✗ Born in Riyadh, my family moved to Canada when I was nine. → ✓ Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. (Same subject in both parts.)"],"arabic":"جملة اسم الفاعل (ing) تدل على فعل مبني للمعلوم في الوقت نفسه: Arriving in January, we were shocked.\nجملة اسم المفعول (ed) تدل على معنى المجهول: Encouraged by a teacher, I joined the club.\nhaving + التصريف الثالث لحدث سابق، وhaving been + التصريف الثالث لحدث سابق مبني للمجهول، والنفي not + الصيغة.\nيجب أن يكون الفاعل واحدًا في جزأي الجملة، وإلا صار المُعدِّل معلّقًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Spent Five Years Abroad…: Participle Clauses”. Let’s learn participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U1-LS1 | Having Spent Five Years Abroad…: Participle Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Between Two Worlds – Lina”: Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. My father, having won a scholarship to study engineering, had to spend five years at a university in Toronto. Arriving in January, we were shocked by the cold. Knowing only a little English, I felt completely lost at school. Sitting alone at lunch, I often wondered whether I would ever belong. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. My father, having won a scholarship to study engineering, had to spend five years at a university in Toronto. Arriving in January, we were shocked by the cold. Knowing only a little English, I felt completely lost at school. Sitting alone at lunch, I often wondered whether I would ever belong.","visual":"Image GV-L09-U1-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A participle clause replaces a full clause and makes writing more concise. A present participle (-ing) shows an active action at the same time as the main verb: Arriving in January, we were shocked. (= When we arrived…) A past participle (-ed/-en) has a passive meaning: Encouraged by a teacher, I joined the club. (= Because I was encouraged…) A perfect participle (having + past participle) shows that the action happened before the main action: Having read the same novels, I had something to talk about. The perfect passive (having been + past participle) is for an earlier passive action: Having been raised by his grandparents, he loved old stories. Make negatives with not before the participle: Not knowing anyone, I sat alone.","on_screen":"-ing = active, same time • -ed/-en = passive • having + past participle = earlier action • having been + past participle = earlier passive action","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Raised in a bilingual family, Lina speaks Arabic and English fluently. Looking at old photos, my grandfather told us about his childhood. Having lived in four countries, Omar adapts easily to new places.","on_screen":"Raised in a bilingual family, Lina speaks Arabic and English fluently. / Looking at old photos, my grandfather told us about his childhood. / Having lived in four countries, Omar adapts easily to new places.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Having been lived abroad, he speaks English well.” That’s not right. We say: “Having lived abroad, he speaks English well.” Active earlier action → having + past participle.","on_screen":"✗ Having been lived abroad, he speaks English well.   ✓ Having lived abroad, he speaks English well.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Having read the novels, I had something to talk about.\" Which came first? … Think! … Reading the novels. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Having spent five years abroad, I had changed.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Having spent five years abroad, I had changed.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","جملة اسم الفاعل (ing) تدل على فعل مبني للمعلوم في الوقت نفسه: Arriving in January, we were shocked.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Having been lived abroad, he speaks English well. — الصحيح: Having lived abroad, he speaks English well.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Having spent five years abroad, I had changed."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Between Two Worlds – Lina","body":"Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. My father, having won a scholarship to study engineering, had to spend five years at a university in Toronto.\nArriving in January, we were shocked by the cold. Knowing only a little English, I felt completely lost at school. Sitting alone at lunch, I often wondered whether I would ever belong.\nThings changed slowly. Encouraged by a patient teacher, I joined the school's book club. Having read the same novels as my classmates, I finally had something to talk about. By the end of the first year, I had made two close friends, one from Korea and one from Brazil. Having moved countries ourselves, we understood each other perfectly.\nWhen we returned to Riyadh, I expected to feel at home immediately. Instead, I felt like a visitor. Having spent five years abroad, I had changed more than I realized. My cousins laughed at my accent, and some traditions felt unfamiliar.\nToday, looking back, I see my experience as a gift. Belonging, I have learned, is not only about where you were born; it is about the people who accept you. Speaking two languages and understanding two cultures, I can build bridges between different worlds.","word_count":208,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ in January, we were shocked by the cold.","options":["To arrive","Having been arrived","Arrived","Arriving"],"answer":"Arriving","explanation":"Present participle (-ing): active, same time.","explanation_ar":"اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"___ by a patient teacher, Lina joined the book club.","options":["To encourage","Encouraging","Encouraged","Having encouraged"],"answer":"Encouraged","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"___ five years abroad, Lina had changed more than she realized.","options":["Having spent","Having been spent","To have spending","Spent"],"answer":"Having spent","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ by his grandparents, he grew up loving traditional stories.","options":["Raising","Having been raised","Having raised","Being raise"],"answer":"Having been raised","explanation":"Perfect passive (having been + past participle).","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"___ anyone at the new school, I sat alone at lunch.","options":["Not knew","No knowing","Knowing not","Not knowing"],"answer":"Not knowing","explanation":"Negative: not + participle.","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"______ in Kairouan around 800, Fatima al-Fihri later moved to Fez, where she founded one of the world's oldest universities.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Having born","Born","Bearing","To be born"],"answer":"Born","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct participle form. Word bank: Not wanting, Surrounded, Having finished","items":[{"q":"______ (finish) his studies, my uncle returned to Riyadh.","options":[],"answer":"Having finished","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"______ (surround) by unfamiliar faces, Saad felt nervous.","options":[],"answer":"Surrounded","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"______ (not / want) to disturb anyone, she left quietly.","options":[],"answer":"Not wanting","explanation":"Negative: not + participle.","explanation_ar":"النفي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with a participle clause.","items":[{"q":"Because he had lived in four countries, Omar adapted easily.  (perfect participle)","options":[],"answer":"Having lived in four countries, Omar adapted easily.","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"When she looked at old photos, Grandma remembered her childhood.  (present participle)","options":[],"answer":"Looking at old photos, Grandma remembered her childhood.","explanation":"Present participle (-ing): active, same time.","explanation_ar":"اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"Because I didn't understand the joke, I just smiled.  (negative)","options":[],"answer":"Not understanding the joke, I just smiled.","explanation":"Negative: not + participle.","explanation_ar":"النفي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write about identity.","items":[{"q":"Begin a sentence with \"Born in…\".","options":[],"answer":"Born in Taif, I have always loved the mountains.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with \"Having…\" about an experience that changed you.","options":[],"answer":"Having volunteered at a hospital, I now want to study nursing.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with \"Not knowing…\".","options":[],"answer":"Not knowing the way, we asked a shopkeeper for directions.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin a sentence with a past participle (Encouraged / Inspired / Raised…).","options":[],"answer":"Inspired by my grandfather, I started learning calligraphy.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"Having been…\".","options":[],"answer":"Having been chosen as captain, Faisal felt proud and nervous.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: My identity story.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about where you come from using participle clauses.","options":[],"answer":"Born in Abha, I grew up surrounded by green mountains. Raised in a big family, I learned to share everything.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about a time you felt you didn't belong.","options":[],"answer":"Arriving at my new school, I didn't know anyone. Not wanting to look shy, I pretended to read a book.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about how you feel now, with a participle clause.","options":[],"answer":"Having made many friends, I now feel completely at home.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Ball Toss Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Students stand in a circle. The teacher says a cue word and throws the ball.","The catcher makes a sentence with the cue word and participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same.","If it is correct, the catcher chooses the next cue word and throws the ball to a new student.","If it is not correct, classmates help fix it, and the same student tries again."],"data":[{"label":"Born in Riyadh, …"},{"label":"Having moved abroad, …"},{"label":"Not knowing anyone, …"},{"label":"Encouraged by…, …"},{"label":"Arriving in January, …"},{"label":"Having been raised…, …"},{"label":"Feeling homesick, …"},{"label":"…, leaving him…"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ in a bilingual family, Noura speaks Arabic and English fluently.","options":["To raise","Raising","Raised","Having raised"],"answer":"Raised","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"___ at old family photos, my father told us about his childhood.","options":["To have looked","Having been looked","Looked","Looking"],"answer":"Looking","explanation":"Present participle (-ing): active, same time.","explanation_ar":"اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"___ the exam, the students relaxed in the garden.","options":["Finished by","Having been finished","To finish","Having finished"],"answer":"Having finished","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ twice by the committee, the young artist finally had his work accepted.","options":["Having been rejected","Having rejected","Being reject","Rejecting"],"answer":"Having been rejected","explanation":"Perfect passive (having been + past participle).","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"","options":["Not wanted to be late, she took a taxi.","Wanting not to be late, she took a taxi.","Not wanting to be late, she took a taxi.","No wanting to be late, she took a taxi."],"answer":"Not wanting to be late, she took a taxi.","explanation":"Negative: not + participle.","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"","options":["Having lived in Egypt for years, my aunt's Egyptian Arabic is perfect.","Having lived in Egypt for years, Egyptian Arabic is easy for my aunt.","Having lived in Egypt for years, the language comes naturally.","Having lived in Egypt for years, my aunt speaks Egyptian Arabic perfectly."],"answer":"Having lived in Egypt for years, my aunt speaks Egyptian Arabic perfectly.","explanation":"Same subject in both parts.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل نفسه."},{"q":"___ three languages, she works as an interpreter at the airport.","options":["Spoken","Speaking","To speak","Having been spoken"],"answer":"Speaking","explanation":"Present participle (-ing): active, same time.","explanation_ar":"اسم الفاعل."},{"q":"______ by the warmth of his new classmates, the exchange student soon felt that he belonged.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Having welcomed","Welcomed","Welcoming","To welcome"],"answer":"Welcomed","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"______ more than twenty years in London, the Saudi novelist still writes about the villages of his childhood in the Asir mountains.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["To live","Having been lived","Having lived","Lived"],"answer":"Having lived","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"In \"Having moved countries ourselves, we understood each other,\" which action happened first?","options":["moving countries","understanding each other","We can't tell.","They happened at the same time."],"answer":"moving countries","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A split illustration: on the left, Lina as a nine-year-old in a snowy Canadian schoolyard, holding a book and looking lost; on the right, Lina at sixteen in Riyadh, laughing with cousins at a family gathering; a bridge connecting the two halves. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A split illustration: on the left, Lina as a nine-year-old in a snowy Canadian schoolyard, holding a book and looking lost; on the right, Lina at sixteen in Riyadh, laughing with cousins at a family gathering; a bridge connecting the two halves"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle)"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows identity (who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing identity: who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows belong (to feel happy and accepted in a place or group); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing belong: to feel happy and accepted in a place or group"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows abroad (in or to a foreign country); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing abroad: in or to a foreign country"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows unfamiliar (not known or not experienced before); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing unfamiliar: not known or not experienced before"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Ball Toss Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Ball Toss Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W01","script":"identity. identity. Language is an important part of our identity.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W02","script":"belong. belong. After a few months, I finally felt that I belonged.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W03","script":"abroad. abroad. My brother is studying abroad in Malaysia.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-W04","script":"unfamiliar. unfamiliar. Everything in the new city felt unfamiliar.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-T1","script":"Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. My father, having won a scholarship to study engineering, had to spend five years at a university in Toronto. Arriving in January, we were shocked by the cold. Knowing only a little English, I felt completely lost at school. Sitting alone at lunch, I often wondered whether I would ever belong. Things changed slowly. Encouraged by a patient teacher, I joined the school's book club. Having read the same novels as my classmates, I finally had something to talk about. By the end of the first year, I had made two close friends, one from Korea and one from Brazil. Having moved countries ourselves, we understood each other perfectly. When we returned to Riyadh, I expected to feel at home immediately. Instead, I felt like a visitor. Having spent five years abroad, I had changed more than I realized. My cousins laughed at my accent, and some traditions felt unfamiliar. Today, looking back, I see my experience as a gift. Belonging, I have learned, is not only about where you were born; it is about the people who accept you. Speaking two languages and understanding two cultures, I can build bridges between different worlds.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Spent Five Years Abroad…: Participle Clauses”. Let’s learn participle clauses: present (-ing, active, same time), past (-ed/-en, passive), perfect (having + past participle, earlier action), perfect passive (having been + past participle), negative (not + participle); the subject of the participle and the main clause must be the same together. Listen to the start of “Between Two Worlds – Lina”: Born in Riyadh, I moved to Canada with my family when I was nine. My father, having won a scholarship to study engineering, had to spend five years at a university in Toronto. Arriving in January, we were shocked by the cold. Knowing only a little English, I felt completely lost at school. Sitting alone at lunch, I often wondered whether I would ever belong. Look at the green words. A participle clause replaces a full clause and makes writing more concise. A present participle (-ing) shows an active action at the same time as the main verb: Arriving in January, we were shocked. (= When we arrived…) A past participle (-ed/-en) has a passive meaning: Encouraged by a teacher, I joined the club. (= Because I was encouraged…) A perfect participle (having + past participle) shows that the action happened before the main action: Having read the same novels, I had something to talk about. The perfect passive (having been + past participle) is for an earlier passive action: Having been raised by his grandparents, he loved old stories. Make negatives with not before the participle: Not knowing anyone, I sat alone. Here are some examples. Raised in a bilingual family, Lina speaks Arabic and English fluently. Looking at old photos, my grandfather told us about his childhood. Having lived in four countries, Omar adapts easily to new places. Be careful! Some learners say: “Having been lived abroad, he speaks English well.” That’s not right. We say: “Having lived abroad, he speaks English well.” Active earlier action → having + past participle. Quick check: \"Having read the novels, I had something to talk about.\" Which came first? … Think! … Reading the novels. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Having spent five years abroad, I had changed.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2","title":"Feeling Homesick, He Called His Mother: Participle Clauses for Time, Reason, Result and Condition","objectives":["Students will be able to use participle clauses to express time, reason, result and condition.","Students will be able to use conjunctions and prepositions with participles (after leaving, when asked, on arriving) for clarity.","I can explain the meaning of \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother\" (reason).","I can write \"After leaving school, I…\" and \"…, leaving him exhausted.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول للتعبير عن الزمن والسبب والنتيجة والشرط.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام أدوات الربط وحروف الجر مع هذه الصيغ (after leaving, when asked, on arriving) للوضوح.","أستطيع أن أشرح معنى Feeling homesick, he called his mother (السبب).","أستطيع أن أكتب After leaving school, I… و…, leaving him exhausted."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"adapt","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈdæpt/","definition":"to change your behavior to deal with a new situation","arabic":"يتأقلم","example":"Children usually adapt quickly to a new school.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W01"},{"word":"accent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əkˈsɛnt/","definition":"the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from","arabic":"لكنة","example":"He speaks English with a slight Saudi accent.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W02"},{"word":"diversity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈvɝsɪti/","definition":"the fact of including many different types of people or things","arabic":"التنوّع","example":"Our school is proud of its cultural diversity.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W03"},{"word":"tolerance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɑlɚəns/","definition":"willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own","arabic":"التسامح","example":"Tolerance helps people from different backgrounds live together.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Participle clauses can express different meanings. Time (= when, after, while): Arriving at the airport, we saw snow. Reason (= because, as): Feeling homesick, he called his mother. Being the youngest, she was spoiled by everyone. Condition (= if): Used carefully, social media can connect people.\nA participle clause at the end of a sentence often shows a result: The storm destroyed the bridge, leaving the village isolated. She won the scholarship, making her parents very proud. Use a comma before it.\nTo make the meaning clearer, you can put a conjunction or preposition before the participle: after leaving school, while living abroad, before moving, since joining the club, when asked, if used correctly, on arriving (= as soon as we arrived). The subject must still be the same in both parts.\ntime: Arriving…, / After leaving…, / On arriving…,\nreason: Feeling…, / Being…, / Not knowing…,\ncondition: Used carefully, … / If treated well, …\nresult: …, leaving / causing / making…","examples":["After leaving school, my father joined the air force.","Being new to the city, I got lost easily.","On arriving in Madinah, we went straight to the Prophet's Mosque.","When asked about her roots, she proudly talks about her village.","The flood damaged many homes, forcing families to move.","Used wisely, technology can bring families closer.","While studying abroad, he missed his mother's cooking.","Since joining the club, Adam has made many friends."],"common_errors":["✗ After leave school, he became a pilot. → ✓ After leaving school, he became a pilot. (Preposition + -ing.)","✗ When asking about his family, he smiled. (someone asked him) → ✓ When asked about his family, he smiled. (Passive meaning → past participle.)","✗ The earthquake destroyed the town, left thousands homeless. → ✓ The earthquake destroyed the town, leaving thousands homeless. (Result → -ing.)","✗ Being rainy, we stayed inside. → ✓ Because it was rainy, we stayed inside. / It being rainy, we stayed inside. (We weren't rainy – the subjects don't match.)"],"arabic":"جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول تعبّر عن معانٍ مختلفة: الزمن (Arriving at the airport…)، والسبب (Feeling homesick…)، والشرط (Used carefully…).\nفي نهاية الجملة بعد فاصلة تعبّر غالبًا عن النتيجة: The storm destroyed the bridge, leaving the village isolated.\nيمكن إضافة أداة ربط أو حرف جر للوضوح: after leaving، while living، when asked، on arriving.\nيجب أن يبقى الفاعل واحدًا في جزأي الجملة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Feeling Homesick, He Called His Mother: Participle Clauses for Time, Reason, Result and Condition”. Let’s learn the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U1-LS2 | Feeling Homesick, He Called His Mother: Participle Clauses for Time, Reason, Result and Condition","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: While living in Canada, she learned to ski. Not having a passport, he couldn't travel. (Participle clauses are not used to form questions.)","on_screen":"While living in Canada, she learned to ski. | Not having a passport, he couldn't travel. | (Participle clauses are not used to form questions.)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Participle clauses can express different meanings. Time (= when, after, while): Arriving at the airport, we saw snow. Reason (= because, as): Feeling homesick, he called his mother. Being the youngest, she was spoiled by everyone. Condition (= if): Used carefully, social media can connect people. A participle clause at the end of a sentence often shows a result: The storm destroyed the bridge, leaving the village isolated. She won the scholarship, making her parents very proud. Use a comma before it.","on_screen":"time: Arriving…, / After leaving…, / On arriving…, • reason: Feeling…, / Being…, / Not knowing…, • condition: Used carefully, … / If treated well, …","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. After leaving school, my father joined the air force. Being new to the city, I got lost easily. On arriving in Madinah, we went straight to the Prophet's Mosque.","on_screen":"After leaving school, my father joined the air force. / Being new to the city, I got lost easily. / On arriving in Madinah, we went straight to the Prophet's Mosque.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “After leave school, he became a pilot.” That’s not right. We say: “After leaving school, he became a pilot.” Preposition + -ing.","on_screen":"✗ After leave school, he became a pilot.   ✓ After leaving school, he became a pilot.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother.\" Why did he call? … Think! … Because he felt homesick (reason). Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the meaning of \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother\" (reason).","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain the meaning of \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother\" (reason).","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول تعبّر عن معانٍ مختلفة: الزمن (Arriving at the airport…)، والسبب (Feeling homesick…)، والشرط (Used carefully…).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: After leave school, he became a pilot. — الصحيح: After leaving school, he became a pilot.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح معنى Feeling homesick, he called his mother (السبب)."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ homesick, Saad called his mother every evening.","options":["Felt","To feel","Having been felt","Feeling"],"answer":"Feeling","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"___ in Madinah, we went straight to the Prophet's Mosque.","options":["On arrived","On arriving","In arrive","At arriving"],"answer":"On arriving","explanation":"Conjunction or preposition + participle.","explanation_ar":"أداة الربط + الصيغة."},{"q":"The flood damaged hundreds of homes, ___ families to move.","options":["forcing","forced","to force","having forced"],"answer":"forcing","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"___ wisely, social media can bring families closer.","options":["To use","Having used","Using","Used"],"answer":"Used","explanation":"Condition (if).","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"When ___ about her roots, Lina proudly talks about her grandparents' village.","options":["asked","having asked","asking","to ask"],"answer":"asked","explanation":"Conjunction or preposition + participle.","explanation_ar":"أداة الربط + الصيغة."},{"q":"Many international students struggle during their first winter in Canada, ______ them to buy warm clothes they have never needed before.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["to have forced","force","forcing","forced"],"answer":"forcing","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: What does each participle clause express? Write time, reason, condition or result. Word bank: condition, result, reason","items":[{"q":"Being the oldest child, Faisal helped his parents a lot. → ______","options":[],"answer":"reason","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"The heavy rain flooded the roads, causing long delays. → ______","options":[],"answer":"result","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"Stored in a cool place, dates can last for months. → ______","options":[],"answer":"condition","explanation":"Condition (if).","explanation_ar":"الشرط."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite with a participle clause.","items":[{"q":"After he left school, my father joined the air force. (After + -ing)  (time)","options":[],"answer":"After leaving school, my father joined the air force.","explanation":"Time (when / after / while / on + -ing).","explanation_ar":"الزمن."},{"q":"Because she was new to the city, she got lost easily. (Being…)  (reason)","options":[],"answer":"Being new to the city, she got lost easily.","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"The team won the final, and this made the whole school proud. (…, making…)  (result)","options":[],"answer":"The team won the final, making the whole school proud.","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Explain your experiences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"While living / studying…\".","options":[],"answer":"While living in Jeddah, I learned to swim in the sea.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"Being…\" to give a reason.","options":[],"answer":"Being the only girl among four brothers, I learned to play football.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence ending with a result clause (leaving / making…).","options":[],"answer":"My cousin moved to Dubai, leaving me without my best friend.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a condition (Used / Taken / Treated…).","options":[],"answer":"Treated with respect, newcomers soon feel at home.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"Since joining…\".","options":[],"answer":"Since joining the scout group, I have become more confident.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A newcomer's guide.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 tips for new students using participle clauses of condition or time.","options":[],"answer":"Arriving early on the first day, you can find your classroom easily. Asked for help, most students will gladly show you around.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about how a newcomer feels, using reason clauses.","options":[],"answer":"Not knowing anyone, newcomers often feel lonely. Being far from home, they may miss their families.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with a result clause about kindness.","options":[],"answer":"One classmate invited me to sit with him, making my first day much easier.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Dice Sentences","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Turn over a cue card and roll the die.","Make a sentence with the cue in the form shown by the die.","The player on the left checks; a correct sentence wins the card.","The player with the most cards after 7 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"born / in Jeddah / she / speak Arabic and French"},{"cue":"having / move / three times / he / adapt quickly"},{"cue":"not / know / the language / I / feel lost"},{"cue":"encouraged / by her teacher / she / join the club"},{"cue":"arrive / at the airport / we / see snow"},{"cue":"having been raised / by his grandparents / he / love old stories"},{"cue":"feel homesick / he / call his mother"},{"cue":"the storm / destroy / the bridge / leaving / the village isolated"},{"cue":"on / hear / the news / she / cry"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ the youngest in the family, Ziyad gets a lot of attention.","options":["Being","Been","To be","Having being"],"answer":"Being","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"___ in the desert at night, we saw thousands of stars.","options":["Camping","Camped","To camp","Having been camped"],"answer":"Camping","explanation":"Time (when / after / while / on + -ing).","explanation_ar":"الزمن."},{"q":"The earthquake destroyed the town's water supply, ___ thousands without clean water.","options":["left","to leave","leaving","having left them"],"answer":"leaving","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"___ properly, a traditional Sadu rug can last for generations.","options":["Cared for","To care","Caring for","Having cared"],"answer":"Cared for","explanation":"Condition (if).","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"Before ___ to Canada, Lina's family took English lessons.","options":["having been moved","move","moved","moving"],"answer":"moving","explanation":"Conjunction or preposition + participle.","explanation_ar":"أداة الربط + الصيغة."},{"q":"","options":["While wait for the bus, I talked to an old man from Yemen.","While waiting for the bus, I talked to an old man from Yemen.","While waited for the bus, I talked to an old man from Yemen.","While having waited for the bus, an old man from Yemen talked."],"answer":"While waiting for the bus, I talked to an old man from Yemen.","explanation":"Time (when / after / while / on + -ing).","explanation_ar":"الزمن."},{"q":"","options":["No having a visa, he couldn't attend the conference.","Not having a visa, he couldn't attend the conference.","Having not a visa, he couldn't attend the conference.","Not had a visa, he couldn't attend the conference."],"answer":"Not having a visa, he couldn't attend the conference.","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"______ by thousands of volunteers, the national cleanup campaign collected more than fifty tons of litter from beaches and parks.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["To support","Supporting","Supported","Having supported"],"answer":"Supported","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"The new metro line connected the city's eastern and western districts, ______ commuting times by almost half.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["to be cut","cut","having been cut","cutting"],"answer":"cutting","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"\"On hearing the news, she cried.\" What does \"On hearing\" mean?","options":["before she heard","as soon as she heard","because she didn't hear","if she hears"],"answer":"as soon as she heard","explanation":"Time (when / after / while / on + -ing).","explanation_ar":"الزمن."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A university dormitory abroad: Saad on a video call with his mother at night, a plate of homemade dates on his desk; a second panel shows students from different countries cooking together in a shared kitchen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A university dormitory abroad: Saad on a video call with his mother at night, a plate of homemade dates on his desk; a second panel shows students from different countries cooking together in a shared kitchen"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Identity and Belonging”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if)"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows adapt (to change your behavior to deal with a new situation); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing adapt: to change your behavior to deal with a new situation"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows accent (the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing accent: the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows diversity (the fact of including many different types of people or things); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing diversity: the fact of including many different types of people or things"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows tolerance (willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing tolerance: willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Dice Sentences”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Dice Sentences"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W01","script":"adapt. adapt. Children usually adapt quickly to a new school.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W02","script":"accent. accent. He speaks English with a slight Saudi accent.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W03","script":"diversity. diversity. Our school is proud of its cultural diversity.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-W04","script":"tolerance. tolerance. Tolerance helps people from different backgrounds live together.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Feeling Homesick, He Called His Mother: Participle Clauses for Time, Reason, Result and Condition”. Let’s learn the meanings of participle clauses: time (when/after/while), reason (because/as), result (…, leaving / causing / making…), condition (if); conjunction + participle (after leaving, while living, before moving, when asked, on arriving, since joining) together. Remember these patterns: While living in Canada, she learned to ski. Not having a passport, he couldn't travel. (Participle clauses are not used to form questions.) Participle clauses can express different meanings. Time (= when, after, while): Arriving at the airport, we saw snow. Reason (= because, as): Feeling homesick, he called his mother. Being the youngest, she was spoiled by everyone. Condition (= if): Used carefully, social media can connect people. A participle clause at the end of a sentence often shows a result: The storm destroyed the bridge, leaving the village isolated. She won the scholarship, making her parents very proud. Use a comma before it. Here are some examples. After leaving school, my father joined the air force. Being new to the city, I got lost easily. On arriving in Madinah, we went straight to the Prophet's Mosque. Be careful! Some learners say: “After leave school, he became a pilot.” That’s not right. We say: “After leaving school, he became a pilot.” Preposition + -ing. Quick check: \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother.\" Why did he call? … Think! … Because he felt homesick (reason). Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the meaning of \"Feeling homesick, he called his mother\" (reason).","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3","title":"Identity, Childhood, Solidarity: Abstract Nouns and Suffixes","objectives":["Students will be able to form abstract nouns from adjectives, verbs and nouns using suffixes.","Students will be able to use identity and belonging vocabulary precisely in context.","I can change \"diverse\" into \"diversity\" and \"child\" into \"childhood\".","I can talk about identity with words like tolerance, solidarity and roots."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تكوين الأسماء المجردة من الصفات والأفعال والأسماء باستخدام اللواحق.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام مفردات الهوية والانتماء بدقة في السياق.","أستطيع أن أحوّل diverse إلى diversity وchild إلى childhood.","أستطيع أن أتحدث عن الهوية بكلمات مثل tolerance وsolidarity وroots."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"nationality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌnæʃəˈnæləti/","definition":"the legal right of belonging to a particular country","arabic":"الجنسية","example":"Students of twenty nationalities study at the university.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W01"},{"word":"citizenship","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪtɪzənˌʃɪp/","definition":"being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities","arabic":"المواطنة","example":"Good citizenship means caring about your community.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W02"},{"word":"solidarity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsɑləˈdɛrəti/","definition":"support and unity between people who share a goal or situation","arabic":"التضامن","example":"The fans showed solidarity with the injured player.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W03"},{"word":"adolescence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌædəˈlɛsəns/","definition":"the period of life between childhood and adulthood","arabic":"المراهقة","example":"Adolescence is a time of big physical and emotional changes.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W04"},{"word":"acceptance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ækˈsɛptəns/","definition":"the act of being welcomed into a group","arabic":"القبول","example":"Every new student needs acceptance and friendship.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W05"},{"word":"roots","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ruts/","definition":"the place, family and culture you come from","arabic":"الجذور / الأصول","example":"Although he lives in London, he never forgets his roots.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W06"},{"word":"bilingual","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/baɪˈlɪŋɡwəl/","definition":"able to speak two languages well","arabic":"ثنائي اللغة","example":"Lina is bilingual in Arabic and English.","image_id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings and qualities that you can't touch: identity, belonging, tolerance, diversity, freedom, childhood. Many are formed with suffixes: -ness (kind → kindness, aware → awareness), -ity (diverse → diversity, national → nationality, solid → solidarity), -hood (child → childhood, neighbor → neighborhood), -ship (citizen → citizenship, friend → friendship), -dom (free → freedom, wise → wisdom).\n-ance / -ence come from adjectives ending in -ant / -ent: tolerant → tolerance, independent → independence, adolescent → adolescence. -ment and -ion form nouns from verbs: accept → acceptance; achieve → achievement; connect → connection.\nWatch the spelling changes: happy → happiness; wise → wisdom; generous → generosity; curious → curiosity; able → ability. Use a dictionary to check unfamiliar forms.\nCollocations/phrases: -ness: kindness • awareness • loneliness • happiness • homesickness | -ity: identity • diversity • nationality • solidarity • curiosity • generosity | -hood / -ship / -dom: childhood • neighborhood • citizenship • friendship • freedom • wisdom | -ance / -ence / -ment: tolerance • acceptance • independence • adolescence • achievement","examples":["Cultural diversity makes our city interesting.","My grandmother often talks about her childhood in the village.","Tolerance and respect help neighbors live in peace.","The class showed solidarity with the student who lost his father.","Good citizenship includes volunteering and obeying the law.","Adolescence is a time when many people search for their identity.","Loneliness is common among students who study abroad.","Bilingual children often switch languages without thinking."],"common_errors":["✗ Our school is proud of its divers. → ✓ Our school is proud of its diversity. (Noun → diversity.)","✗ I had a happy childness. → ✓ I had a happy childhood. (child + hood.)","✗ She showed great generousness. → ✓ She showed great generosity. (generous → generosity.)","✗ The students showed solidity with their friend. → ✓ The students showed solidarity with their friend. (solidarity = support and unity.)"],"arabic":"الأسماء المجردة تسمّي الأفكار والمشاعر والصفات: identity (هوية) وtolerance (تسامح) وdiversity (تنوّع) وfreedom (حرية).\nاللواحق: ness (kindness) وity (diversity) وhood (childhood) وship (citizenship) وdom (freedom) وance / ence (tolerance / independence).\nانتبه لتغيّر التهجئة: happy → happiness، generous → generosity، wise → wisdom.\nsolidarity تعني التضامن، وroots تعني الجذور والأصول."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Identity, Childhood, Solidarity: Abstract Nouns and Suffixes”. Let’s learn abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U1-LS3 | Identity, Childhood, Solidarity: Abstract Nouns and Suffixes","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. nationality: the legal right of belonging to a particular country. citizenship: being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities. solidarity: support and unity between people who share a goal or situation.","on_screen":"nationality | citizenship | solidarity","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings and qualities that you can't touch: identity, belonging, tolerance, diversity, freedom, childhood. Many are formed with suffixes: -ness (kind → kindness, aware → awareness), -ity (diverse → diversity, national → nationality, solid → solidarity), -hood (child → childhood, neighbor → neighborhood), -ship (citizen → citizenship, friend → friendship), -dom (free → freedom, wise → wisdom). -ance / -ence come from adjectives ending in -ant / -ent: tolerant → tolerance, independent → independence, adolescent → adolescence. -ment and -ion form nouns from verbs: accept → acceptance; achieve → achievement; connect → connection.","on_screen":"abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Cultural diversity makes our city interesting. My grandmother often talks about her childhood in the village. Tolerance and respect help neighbors live in peace.","on_screen":"Cultural diversity makes our city interesting. / My grandmother often talks about her childhood in the village. / Tolerance and respect help neighbors live in peace.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Our school is proud of its divers.” That’s not right. We say: “Our school is proud of its diversity.” Noun → diversity.","on_screen":"✗ Our school is proud of its divers.   ✓ Our school is proud of its diversity.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which suffix turns \"child\" into a noun meaning \"the time of being a child\"? … Think! … *-hood* – childhood. Great job! Now you can say: I can change \"diverse\" into \"diversity\" and \"child\" into \"childhood\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can change \"diverse\" into \"diversity\" and \"child\" into \"childhood\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الأسماء المجردة تسمّي الأفكار والمشاعر والصفات: identity (هوية) وtolerance (تسامح) وdiversity (تنوّع) وfreedom (حرية).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Our school is proud of its divers. — الصحيح: Our school is proud of its diversity.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أحوّل diverse إلى diversity وchild إلى childhood."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"Our school is proud of its cultural ___.","options":["diverse","diversity","diversify","diversely"],"answer":"diversity","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"My grandfather often talks about his ___ in a small village near Al-Baha.","options":["childdom","childhood","childship","childness"],"answer":"childhood","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"People from different backgrounds can live in peace if they show ___.","options":["tolerant","tolerantly","tolerance","tolerate"],"answer":"tolerance","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Lina speaks Arabic and English equally well. She is ___.","options":["unfamiliar","abroad","adolescent","bilingual"],"answer":"bilingual","explanation":"Choose the identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"She showed great ___ when she gave her savings to the charity.","options":["generous","generousity","generosity","generousness"],"answer":"generosity","explanation":"Spelling changes with suffixes.","explanation_ar":"تغيّر التهجئة."},{"q":"When the factory closed, workers from other cities traveled to join the protest and support their colleagues, showing remarkable ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["solidarity","nationality","adolescence","loneliness"],"answer":"solidarity","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"identity","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"who you are and what makes you different","explanation":"identity → who you are and what makes you different. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"citizenship","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"being a legal member of a country with rights and duties","explanation":"citizenship → being a legal member of a country with rights and duties. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"adolescence","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"the period between childhood and adulthood","explanation":"adolescence → the period between childhood and adulthood. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"roots","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"the family and culture you come from","explanation":"roots → the family and culture you come from. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"acceptance","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"being welcomed into a group","explanation":"acceptance → being welcomed into a group. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"nationality","options":["being welcomed into a group","the period between childhood and adulthood","the family and culture you come from","the country you legally belong to","who you are and what makes you different","being a legal member of a country with rights and duties"],"answer":"the country you legally belong to","explanation":"nationality → the country you legally belong to. Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about identity.","items":[{"q":"What three things are most important to your identity?","options":[],"answer":"My faith, my family and my language are the most important parts of my identity.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Describe a happy memory from your childhood.","options":[],"answer":"In my childhood, I loved visiting my grandparents' farm in Al-Ahsa.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How does your school show diversity?","options":[],"answer":"Our school has teachers from eight different countries.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"independence\".","options":[],"answer":"Studying abroad gave my brother more independence.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence about your roots.","options":[],"answer":"My roots are in Najran, where my great-grandfather was a farmer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A belonging poster.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about what makes people feel they belong, using 3 abstract nouns.","options":[],"answer":"Kindness makes newcomers feel welcome. Friendship gives them support. Acceptance helps them feel at home.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences with nouns ending in -ity.","options":[],"answer":"Curiosity helps us learn about other cultures. Diversity makes our classroom richer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about good citizenship.","options":[],"answer":"Good citizenship means protecting public places and helping others.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Vocabulary Bingo","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Students write nine words from the list in their grid.","The teacher (or a student caller) reads a definition or Arabic meaning.","Students cross out the matching word.","Three in a row = \"Bingo!\" The winner must read the words and say what each means."],"data":[{"word":"identity","match":"who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different","arabic":"هوية"},{"word":"belong","match":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or group","arabic":"ينتمي"},{"word":"abroad","match":"in or to a foreign country","arabic":"في الخارج"},{"word":"unfamiliar","match":"not known or not experienced before","arabic":"غير مألوف"},{"word":"adapt","match":"to change your behavior to deal with a new situation","arabic":"يتأقلم"},{"word":"accent","match":"the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from","arabic":"لكنة"},{"word":"diversity","match":"the fact of including many different types of people or things","arabic":"التنوّع"},{"word":"tolerance","match":"willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own","arabic":"التسامح"},{"word":"nationality","match":"the legal right of belonging to a particular country","arabic":"الجنسية"},{"word":"citizenship","match":"being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities","arabic":"المواطنة"},{"word":"solidarity","match":"support and unity between people who share a goal or situation","arabic":"التضامن"},{"word":"adolescence","match":"the period of life between childhood and adulthood","arabic":"المراهقة"},{"word":"acceptance","match":"the act of being welcomed into a group","arabic":"القبول"},{"word":"roots","match":"the place, family and culture you come from","arabic":"الجذور / الأصول"},{"word":"bilingual","match":"able to speak two languages well","arabic":"ثنائي اللغة"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Living far from family can cause feelings of ___.","options":["lonelity","lonelily","loneliness","lonely"],"answer":"loneliness","explanation":"Spelling changes with suffixes.","explanation_ar":"تغيّر التهجئة."},{"q":"The UAE, Saudi Arabia and Oman share many traditions but have different ___.","options":["nationalities","national","nationalize","nationally"],"answer":"nationalities","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"As a teenager, my brother wanted more ___ from our parents.","options":["independently","independence","independency","independent"],"answer":"independence","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The old man's ___ came from a long life of experience.","options":["wiseness","wisehood","wiseship","wisdom"],"answer":"wisdom","explanation":"Spelling changes with suffixes.","explanation_ar":"تغيّر التهجئة."},{"q":"Our ___ is safe and friendly; everyone knows each other.","options":["neighborness","neighborship","neighborhood","neighbordom"],"answer":"neighborhood","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"Moving to a new country requires the ability to ___ to new customs.","options":["adopt","adept","adore","adapt"],"answer":"adapt","explanation":"Choose the identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Although he grew up in London, he speaks Arabic with a slight Najdi ___.","options":["roots","accent","tolerance","identity"],"answer":"accent","explanation":"Choose the identity word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"___ is a time of big emotional and physical changes.","options":["Adolescence","Adolesce","Adolescent","Adolescently"],"answer":"Adolescence","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The museum's new exhibition celebrates the ______ of the kingdom's cultures, from the fishing villages of the east to the mountain towns of the south.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["diversity","adolescence","uniformity","loneliness"],"answer":"diversity","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"After years of living abroad, the writer returned to her village to reconnect with her ______, visiting the house where her great-grandparents had lived.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["citizenship","accent","tolerance","roots"],"answer":"roots","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A \"Word Tree of Identity\": roots labelled family, language, faith, homeland; the trunk labelled identity; branches with leaves for abstract nouns (kindness, diversity, childhood, citizenship, freedom, tolerance, independence, acceptance). Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A \"Word Tree of Identity\": roots labelled family, language, faith, homeland; the trunk labelled identity; branches with leaves for abstract nouns (kindness, diversity, childhood, citizenship, freedom, tolerance, independence, acceptance)"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows nationality (the legal right of belonging to a particular country); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing nationality: the legal right of belonging to a particular country"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows citizenship (being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing citizenship: being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows solidarity (support and unity between people who share a goal or situation); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing solidarity: support and unity between people who share a goal or situation"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows adolescence (the period of life between childhood and adulthood); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing adolescence: the period of life between childhood and adulthood"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows acceptance (the act of being welcomed into a group); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing acceptance: the act of being welcomed into a group"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows roots (the place, family and culture you come from); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing roots: the place, family and culture you come from"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows bilingual (able to speak two languages well); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing bilingual: able to speak two languages well"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Vocabulary Bingo”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Vocabulary Bingo"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W01","script":"nationality. nationality. Students of twenty nationalities study at the university.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W02","script":"citizenship. citizenship. Good citizenship means caring about your community.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W03","script":"solidarity. solidarity. The fans showed solidarity with the injured player.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W04","script":"adolescence. adolescence. Adolescence is a time of big physical and emotional changes.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W05","script":"acceptance. acceptance. Every new student needs acceptance and friendship.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W06","script":"roots. roots. Although he lives in London, he never forgets his roots.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-W07","script":"bilingual. bilingual. Lina is bilingual in Arabic and English.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Identity, Childhood, Solidarity: Abstract Nouns and Suffixes”. Let’s learn abstract nouns formed with suffixes -ness (kindness), -ity (diversity, identity), -hood (childhood), -ship (citizenship), -dom (freedom), -ance/-ence (tolerance, independence), -ment (achievement), -ion (connection); word families and spelling changes together. Meet some new words. nationality: the legal right of belonging to a particular country. citizenship: being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities. solidarity: support and unity between people who share a goal or situation. Abstract nouns name ideas, feelings and qualities that you can't touch: identity, belonging, tolerance, diversity, freedom, childhood. Many are formed with suffixes: -ness (kind → kindness, aware → awareness), -ity (diverse → diversity, national → nationality, solid → solidarity), -hood (child → childhood, neighbor → neighborhood), -ship (citizen → citizenship, friend → friendship), -dom (free → freedom, wise → wisdom). -ance / -ence come from adjectives ending in -ant / -ent: tolerant → tolerance, independent → independence, adolescent → adolescence. -ment and -ion form nouns from verbs: accept → acceptance; achieve → achievement; connect → connection. Here are some examples. Cultural diversity makes our city interesting. My grandmother often talks about her childhood in the village. Tolerance and respect help neighbors live in peace. Be careful! Some learners say: “Our school is proud of its divers.” That’s not right. We say: “Our school is proud of its diversity.” Noun → diversity. Quick check: Which suffix turns \"child\" into a noun meaning \"the time of being a child\"? … Think! … *-hood* – childhood. Great job! Now you can say: I can change \"diverse\" into \"diversity\" and \"child\" into \"childhood\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4","title":"Having Moved Abroad, I…: Commas with Participial Phrases and Dangling Participles","objectives":["Students will be able to punctuate introductory, interrupting and final participial phrases correctly.","Students will be able to identify and fix dangling participles by choosing the correct subject.","I can put a comma after \"Having moved abroad,\" and choose the right subject after it.","I can explain why \"The girl sitting by the window is my cousin\" has no commas."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من ترقيم العبارات الوصفية في أول الجملة ووسطها وآخرها بشكل صحيح.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اكتشاف المُعدِّلات المعلّقة وتصحيحها باختيار الفاعل الصحيح.","أستطيع أن أضع فاصلة بعد Having moved abroad, وأختار الفاعل الصحيح بعدها.","أستطيع أن أشرح لماذا لا توجد فواصل في The girl sitting by the window is my cousin."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"identity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈdɛntɪˌti/","definition":"who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different","arabic":"هوية","example":"Language is an important part of our identity.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"belong","pos":"verb","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋ/","definition":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or group","arabic":"ينتمي","example":"After a few months, I finally felt that I belonged.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"abroad","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/əˈbrɔd/","definition":"in or to a foreign country","arabic":"في الخارج","example":"My brother is studying abroad in Malaysia.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"unfamiliar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌʌnfəˈmɪljɚ/","definition":"not known or not experienced before","arabic":"غير مألوف","example":"Everything in the new city felt unfamiliar.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"adapt","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈdæpt/","definition":"to change your behavior to deal with a new situation","arabic":"يتأقلم","example":"Children usually adapt quickly to a new school.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"accent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əkˈsɛnt/","definition":"the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from","arabic":"لكنة","example":"He speaks English with a slight Saudi accent.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Put a comma after an introductory participial phrase: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook. Put commas around a nonessential participial phrase in the middle of a sentence: My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. Use a comma before a participial phrase at the end that adds extra information or a result: The team won the cup, surprising everyone.\nDon't use commas when the participial phrase is essential – it tells us which one: The girl sitting by the window is my cousin. Students living on campus get free meals. (Which students? Only those living on campus.)\nIn SAT questions, the subject right after an introductory participial phrase must be the person or thing doing (or receiving) the action. (not: Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.) (Her English didn't live abroad.) → Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English. Read the phrase, ask \"Who did this?\", and make that the subject.\nIntroductory participial phrase + comma + correct subject\nNonessential phrase in the middle → a pair of commas\nEssential phrase (which one?) → no commas\nSAT: the subject after the comma must do or receive the action","examples":["Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains.","The students, inspired by the speech, started a volunteer club.","The man standing at the door is our new principal.","My brother moved to Riyadh, leaving his old friends behind.","Having finished the project, the team celebrated.","Children raised in two cultures often become bilingual.","Encouraged by her teachers, Noura applied for the scholarship.","The book, written in 1950, is still popular today."],"common_errors":["✗ Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent. → ✓ Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English. (The subject must be the person who lived abroad.)","✗ Raised in Taif Saad loves the mountains. → ✓ Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains. (Comma after the introductory phrase.)","✗ The girl, sitting by the window, is my cousin. (several girls) → ✓ The girl sitting by the window is my cousin. (Essential phrase → no commas.)","✗ My grandfather, born in 1950 still remembers the old souq. → ✓ My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. (Commas in pairs.)"],"arabic":"نضع فاصلة بعد العبارة الوصفية في أول الجملة: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook.\nنضع فاصلتين حول العبارة غير الضرورية في وسط الجملة، ولا نضع فواصل حول العبارة الضرورية التي تحدد أيّ واحد: The girl sitting by the window is my cousin.\nفي أسئلة SAT يجب أن يكون الفاعل بعد الفاصلة هو مَن قام بالفعل أو وقع عليه: Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.\nاسأل: مَن فعل ذلك؟ واجعله الفاعل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Moved Abroad, I…: Commas with Participial Phrases and Dangling Participles”. Let’s learn commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U1-LS4 | Having Moved Abroad, I…: Commas with Participial Phrases and Dangling Participles","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Put a comma after an introductory participial phrase: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook. Put commas around a nonessential participial phrase in the middle of a sentence: My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. Use a comma before a participial phrase at the end that adds extra information or a result: The team won the cup, surprising everyone.","on_screen":"commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Put a comma after an introductory participial phrase: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook. Put commas around a nonessential participial phrase in the middle of a sentence: My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. Use a comma before a participial phrase at the end that adds extra information or a result: The team won the cup, surprising everyone. Don't use commas when the participial phrase is essential – it tells us which one: The girl sitting by the window is my cousin. Students living on campus get free meals. (Which students? Only those living on campus.)","on_screen":"Introductory participial phrase + comma + correct subject • Nonessential phrase in the middle → a pair of commas • Essential phrase (which one?) → no commas","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains. The students, inspired by the speech, started a volunteer club. The man standing at the door is our new principal.","on_screen":"Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains. / The students, inspired by the speech, started a volunteer club. / The man standing at the door is our new principal.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.” That’s not right. We say: “Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.” The subject must be the person who lived abroad.","on_screen":"✗ Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.   ✓ Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.\" Who lived abroad? … Think! … Lina – so *Lina* must be the subject. Great job! Now you can say: I can put a comma after \"Having moved abroad,\" and choose the right subject after it.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can put a comma after \"Having moved abroad,\" and choose the right subject after it.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نضع فاصلة بعد العبارة الوصفية في أول الجملة: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent. — الصحيح: Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أضع فاصلة بعد Having moved abroad, وأختار الفاعل الصحيح بعدها."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Finding My Place (editing passage)","body":"Having moved to a new school the first weeks were hard. My classmates curious about my accent asked me many questions. The teacher , sitting at the front, was not my usual teacher; he was a substitute. Encouraged by my new friends I joined the debate team. Last month, we won the regional final making the whole school proud.","word_count":59,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"","options":["Raised, in Taif Saad loves the mountains.","Raised in Taif Saad, loves the mountains.","Raised in Taif Saad loves the mountains.","Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains."],"answer":"Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains.","explanation":"Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"","options":["My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq.","My grandfather, born in 1950 still remembers the old souq.","My grandfather born in 1950, still remembers the old souq.","My grandfather born, in 1950 still remembers the old souq."],"answer":"My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq.","explanation":"Pair of commas around a nonessential phrase.","explanation_ar":"فاصلتان حول العبارة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"","options":["The students, living on campus get free meals.","The students, living on campus, get free meals.","The students living on campus, get free meals.","The students living on campus get free meals."],"answer":"The students living on campus get free meals.","explanation":"Essential phrase → no commas.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"","options":["Having lived abroad for years, excellent English is spoken by Lina.","Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.","Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.","Having lived abroad for years, English became easy."],"answer":"Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"","options":["Our team, won the cup surprising everyone.","Our team won the cup, surprising everyone.","Our team won, the cup surprising everyone.","Our team won the cup surprising, everyone."],"answer":"Our team won the cup, surprising everyone.","explanation":"Comma before a final participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة قبل العبارة الأخيرة."},{"q":"Having spent three years studying in Japan, ______\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["a deep appreciation for Japanese culture developed in Ahmed.","Japanese culture was deeply appreciated by Ahmed.","Ahmed returned home with a deep appreciation for Japanese culture.","Ahmed's appreciation for Japanese culture grew deeper."],"answer":"Ahmed returned home with a deep appreciation for Japanese culture.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Add commas where they are needed.","items":[{"q":"Encouraged by her teachers Noura applied for the scholarship.  (comma)","options":[],"answer":"Encouraged by her teachers, Noura applied for the scholarship.","explanation":"Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"The book written in 1950 is still popular today. (only one book is meant)  (commas)","options":[],"answer":"The book, written in 1950, is still popular today.","explanation":"Pair of commas around a nonessential phrase.","explanation_ar":"فاصلتان حول العبارة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"Children raised in two cultures often become bilingual.  (no commas)","options":[],"answer":"Children raised in two cultures often become bilingual. (no commas)","explanation":"Essential phrase → no commas.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الضرورية بلا فواصل."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Fix the dangling participles.","items":[{"q":"Arriving at the airport, the snow surprised us.","options":[],"answer":"Arriving at the airport, we were surprised by the snow.","explanation":"Arriving at the airport, the snow surprised us. → Arriving at the airport, we were surprised by the snow.. Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Arriving at the airport, the snow surprised us. → Arriving at the airport, we were surprised by the snow.. الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"Having finished the exam, the classroom was quiet.","options":[],"answer":"Having finished the exam, the students sat quietly in the classroom.","explanation":"Having finished the exam, the classroom was quiet. → Having finished the exam, the students sat quietly in the classroom.. Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Having finished the exam, the classroom was quiet. → Having finished the exam, the students sat quietly in the classroom.. الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"Born in Jeddah, the sea has always been important to him.","options":[],"answer":"Born in Jeddah, he has always loved the sea.","explanation":"Born in Jeddah, the sea has always been important to him. → Born in Jeddah, he has always loved the sea.. Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Born in Jeddah, the sea has always been important to him. → Born in Jeddah, he has always loved the sea.. الفاعل المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the personal essay.","items":[{"q":"Having moved to a new school the first weeks were hard. My classmates, curious about my accent, asked me many questions.","options":[],"answer":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates, curious about my accent, asked me many questions.","explanation":"Having moved to a new school the first weeks were hard. → Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard.. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Having moved to a new school the first weeks were hard. → Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard.. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates curious about my accent asked me many questions.","options":[],"answer":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates, curious about my accent, asked me many questions.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates curious about my accent asked me many questions.","options":[],"answer":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates, curious about my accent, asked me many questions.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"The teacher , sitting at the front, was not my usual teacher; he was a substitute. Encouraged by my new friends, I joined the debate team.","options":[],"answer":"The teacher sitting at the front was not my usual teacher; he was a substitute. Encouraged by my new friends, I joined the debate team.","explanation":", sitting at the front, → sitting at the front. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , sitting at the front, → sitting at the front. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"The teacher sitting at the front was not my usual teacher; he was a substitute. Encouraged by my new friends I joined the debate team.","options":[],"answer":"The teacher sitting at the front was not my usual teacher; he was a substitute. Encouraged by my new friends, I joined the debate team.","explanation":"Encouraged by my new friends I → Encouraged by my new friends, I. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Encouraged by my new friends I → Encouraged by my new friends, I. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates curious about my accent asked me many questions.","options":[],"answer":"Having moved to a new school, I found the first weeks hard. My classmates, curious about my accent, asked me many questions.","explanation":"(add) → ,. Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: (add) → ,. الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write correct sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence starting with a participial phrase about your family.","options":[],"answer":"Raised by hardworking parents, I learned to value every riyal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with a nonessential participial phrase in the middle.","options":[],"answer":"My aunt, trained as a pharmacist, now owns three pharmacies.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with an essential participial phrase (no commas).","options":[],"answer":"The boy wearing the red jacket is my cousin.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Letter Ladder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The leader chooses a word from the list and draws one blank line per letter.","Teams take turns guessing letters. Correct letters are written on the lines.","Each wrong guess removes one rung of the ladder (start with 6 rungs).","The team that completes the word scores 2 points and uses it in a sentence."],"data":[{"word":"identity","clue":"who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different"},{"word":"diversity","clue":"the fact of including many different types of people or things"},{"word":"tolerance","clue":"willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own"},{"word":"citizenship","clue":"being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities"},{"word":"solidarity","clue":"support and unity between people who share a goal or situation"},{"word":"adolescence","clue":"the period of life between childhood and adulthood"},{"word":"belong","clue":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or group"},{"word":"abroad","clue":"in or to a foreign country"},{"word":"unfamiliar","clue":"not known or not experienced before"},{"word":"adapt","clue":"to change your behavior to deal with a new situation"},{"word":"accent","clue":"the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from"},{"word":"nationality","clue":"the legal right of belonging to a particular country"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["Encouraged by his coach Rakan trained harder.","Encouraged by his coach, Rakan trained harder.","Encouraged by his coach Rakan, trained harder.","Encouraged, by his coach Rakan trained harder."],"answer":"Encouraged by his coach, Rakan trained harder.","explanation":"Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"","options":["The festival organized by students, attracted thousands of visitors.","The festival organized, by students attracted thousands of visitors.","The festival, organized by students attracted thousands of visitors.","The festival, organized by students, attracted thousands of visitors."],"answer":"The festival, organized by students, attracted thousands of visitors.","explanation":"Pair of commas around a nonessential phrase.","explanation_ar":"فاصلتان حول العبارة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"","options":["The man, standing next to the principal, is my father.","The man, standing next to the principal is my father.","The man standing next to the principal, is my father.","The man standing next to the principal is my father."],"answer":"The man standing next to the principal is my father.","explanation":"Essential phrase → no commas.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"","options":["Having practiced for months, confidence filled the speech.","Having practiced for months, Omar gave a confident speech.","Having practiced for months, Omar's speech was confident.","Having practiced for months, the speech was confident."],"answer":"Having practiced for months, Omar gave a confident speech.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"","options":["The storm hit the coast damaging, hundreds of boats.","The storm, hit the coast damaging hundreds of boats.","The storm hit the coast, damaging hundreds of boats.","The storm hit, the coast damaging hundreds of boats."],"answer":"The storm hit the coast, damaging hundreds of boats.","explanation":"Comma before a final participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة قبل العبارة الأخيرة."},{"q":"Which sentence has a dangling participle?","options":["Walking into the hall, I heard a deafening noise.","The noise was deafening when I walked into the hall.","When I walked into the hall, the noise was deafening.","Walking into the hall, the noise was deafening."],"answer":"Walking into the hall, the noise was deafening.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"Why are there no commas in \"Students living on campus get free meals\"?","options":["The phrase is extra information.","The phrase is at the end.","The phrase tells us which students.","The sentence is a question."],"answer":"The phrase tells us which students.","explanation":"Essential phrase → no commas.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Raised in a family of fishermen on the Farasan Islands, ______\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the marine biologist has studied the Red Sea's coral reefs for twenty years.","coral reefs became the marine biologist's passion.","the marine biologist's research has focused on coral reefs.","the Red Sea's coral reefs have been studied by the marine biologist for twenty years."],"answer":"the marine biologist has studied the Red Sea's coral reefs for twenty years.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"The novel ______ tells the story of a family divided between two countries.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":[", first published in 1985,",", first published in 1985","first, published in 1985,","first published in 1985,"],"answer":", first published in 1985,","explanation":"Pair of commas around a nonessential phrase.","explanation_ar":"فاصلتان حول العبارة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"Founded in 2009, ______\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["KAUST's researchers come from more than one hundred countries.","the King Abdullah University of Science and Technology attracts researchers from more than one hundred countries.","researchers from more than one hundred countries are attracted to KAUST.","more than one hundred countries send researchers to KAUST."],"answer":"the King Abdullah University of Science and Technology attracts researchers from more than one hundred countries.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Identity and Belonging” that shows commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Identity and Belonging” that shows commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) in action"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Letter Ladder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Letter Ladder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Having Moved Abroad, I…: Commas with Participial Phrases and Dangling Participles”. Let’s learn commas after introductory participial phrases; commas around nonessential participial phrases in the middle or at the end; no commas with essential participial phrases (the girl sitting by the window); fixing dangling participles (SAT Form, Structure, and Sense) together. Put a comma after an introductory participial phrase: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook. Put commas around a nonessential participial phrase in the middle of a sentence: My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. Use a comma before a participial phrase at the end that adds extra information or a result: The team won the cup, surprising everyone. Put a comma after an introductory participial phrase: Having moved abroad, I learned to cook. Put commas around a nonessential participial phrase in the middle of a sentence: My grandfather, born in 1950, still remembers the old souq. Use a comma before a participial phrase at the end that adds extra information or a result: The team won the cup, surprising everyone. Don't use commas when the participial phrase is essential – it tells us which one: The girl sitting by the window is my cousin. Students living on campus get free meals. (Which students? Only those living on campus.) Here are some examples. Raised in Taif, Saad loves the mountains. The students, inspired by the speech, started a volunteer club. The man standing at the door is our new principal. Be careful! Some learners say: “Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.” That’s not right. We say: “Having lived abroad for years, Lina speaks excellent English.” The subject must be the person who lived abroad. Quick check: \"Having lived abroad for years, Lina's English is excellent.\" Who lived abroad? … Think! … Lina – so *Lina* must be the subject. Great job! Now you can say: I can put a comma after \"Having moved abroad,\" and choose the right subject after it.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5","title":"Where Do I Belong?: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to discuss identity and belonging using participle clauses and abstract nouns.","Students will be able to write a reflective paragraph about belonging with correctly punctuated participial phrases, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can talk about what makes me feel I belong.","I can write a reflective paragraph about identity."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة الهوية والانتماء بجمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول والأسماء المجردة.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة فقرة تأملية عن الانتماء بعبارات وصفية مرقّمة بشكل صحيح، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أتحدث عما يجعلني أشعر بالانتماء.","أستطيع أن أكتب فقرة تأملية عن الهوية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"identity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/aɪˈdɛntɪˌti/","definition":"who a person is; the qualities and background that make someone different","arabic":"هوية","example":"Language is an important part of our identity.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"belong","pos":"verb","ipa":"/bɪˈlɔŋ/","definition":"to feel happy and accepted in a place or group","arabic":"ينتمي","example":"After a few months, I finally felt that I belonged.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"abroad","pos":"adverb","ipa":"/əˈbrɔd/","definition":"in or to a foreign country","arabic":"في الخارج","example":"My brother is studying abroad in Malaysia.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"unfamiliar","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌʌnfəˈmɪljɚ/","definition":"not known or not experienced before","arabic":"غير مألوف","example":"Everything in the new city felt unfamiliar.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"adapt","pos":"verb","ipa":"/əˈdæpt/","definition":"to change your behavior to deal with a new situation","arabic":"يتأقلم","example":"Children usually adapt quickly to a new school.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"accent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əkˈsɛnt/","definition":"the way a person pronounces words, showing where he comes from","arabic":"لكنة","example":"He speaks English with a slight Saudi accent.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"diversity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/dɪˈvɝsɪti/","definition":"the fact of including many different types of people or things","arabic":"التنوّع","example":"Our school is proud of its cultural diversity.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tolerance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈtɑlɚəns/","definition":"willingness to accept beliefs or customs that are different from your own","arabic":"التسامح","example":"Tolerance helps people from different backgrounds live together.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"nationality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌnæʃəˈnæləti/","definition":"the legal right of belonging to a particular country","arabic":"الجنسية","example":"Students of twenty nationalities study at the university.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"citizenship","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsɪtɪzənˌʃɪp/","definition":"being a member of a country with legal rights and responsibilities","arabic":"المواطنة","example":"Good citizenship means caring about your community.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"solidarity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsɑləˈdɛrəti/","definition":"support and unity between people who share a goal or situation","arabic":"التضامن","example":"The fans showed solidarity with the injured player.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"adolescence","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌædəˈlɛsəns/","definition":"the period of life between childhood and adulthood","arabic":"المراهقة","example":"Adolescence is a time of big physical and emotional changes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"acceptance","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ækˈsɛptəns/","definition":"the act of being welcomed into a group","arabic":"القبول","example":"Every new student needs acceptance and friendship.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"roots","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ruts/","definition":"the place, family and culture you come from","arabic":"الجذور / الأصول","example":"Although he lives in London, he never forgets his roots.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"bilingual","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/baɪˈlɪŋɡwəl/","definition":"able to speak two languages well","arabic":"ثنائي اللغة","example":"Lina is bilingual in Arabic and English.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 1: participle clauses (present, past, perfect; time, reason, result, condition), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and logical subjects.\nA reflective paragraph tells a story, explains feelings and states a lesson. Participle clauses make your writing more mature; abstract nouns help you talk about ideas; transitions such as however, as a result, in the end show how your thinking changed.\n✔ Having grown up…, / Surrounded by…, / Not wanting…,\n✔ identity, tolerance, curiosity, friendship, belonging\n✔ comma after an introductory phrase • pairs of commas • no commas for essential phrases\n✔ the subject must do the action in the participle clause","examples":["Having moved schools three times, I know how it feels to be new.","Feeling nervous, I stood at the back of the room.","Welcomed by my classmates, I soon felt at home.","The students sitting near me were from Jordan and Indonesia.","Our teacher, born in Sudan, told us about his childhood.","Curiosity helped me make new friends.","Tolerance means respecting people who are different from us.","In the end, I realized that belonging is a feeling we create together."],"common_errors":["✗ Having grown up in Taif, my identity seemed clear. → ✓ Having grown up in Taif, I thought my identity was clear. (Dangling participle → correct subject.)","✗ Surrounded by new languages I felt unsure. → ✓ Surrounded by new languages, I felt unsure. (Comma after the introductory phrase.)","✗ Our friendship built on tolerance, grew stronger. → ✓ Our friendship, built on tolerance, grew stronger. (Commas in pairs.)"],"arabic":"نراجع جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول بأشكالها ومعانيها، والأسماء المجردة، والفواصل مع العبارات الوصفية، والفاعل المنطقي.\nالفقرة التأملية تروي قصة وتشرح المشاعر وتذكر الدرس المستفاد.\nالروابط مثل however وas a result وin the end تُظهر كيف تغيّر تفكيرك."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Where Do I Belong?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L09-U1-LS5 | Where Do I Belong?: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Identity circles: in groups, each student shares one object (a photo, a word in another language, a family recipe) that represents part of his identity, using one participle clause and one abstract noun. Listeners respond with a question and a sentence of connection (\"Having moved too, I understand…\").","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 1: participle clauses (present, past, perfect; time, reason, result, condition), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and logical subjects. A reflective paragraph tells a story, explains feelings and states a lesson. Participle clauses make your writing more mature; abstract nouns help you talk about ideas; transitions such as however, as a result, in the end show how your thinking changed.","on_screen":"✔ Having grown up…, / Surrounded by…, / Not wanting…, • ✔ identity, tolerance, curiosity, friendship, belonging • ✔ comma after an introductory phrase • pairs of commas • no commas for essential phrases","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Having moved schools three times, I know how it feels to be new. Feeling nervous, I stood at the back of the room. Welcomed by my classmates, I soon felt at home.","on_screen":"Having moved schools three times, I know how it feels to be new. / Feeling nervous, I stood at the back of the room. / Welcomed by my classmates, I soon felt at home.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Having grown up in Taif, my identity seemed clear.” That’s not right. We say: “Having grown up in Taif, I thought my identity was clear.” Dangling participle → correct subject.","on_screen":"✗ Having grown up in Taif, my identity seemed clear.   ✓ Having grown up in Taif, I thought my identity was clear.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “Surrounded by new languages I felt unsure.”? … Think! … It should be “Surrounded by new languages, I felt unsure.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about what makes me feel I belong.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can talk about what makes me feel I belong.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول بأشكالها ومعانيها، والأسماء المجردة، والفواصل مع العبارات الوصفية، والفاعل المنطقي.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Having grown up in Taif, my identity seemed clear. — الصحيح: Having grown up in Taif, I thought my identity was clear.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أتحدث عما يجعلني أشعر بالانتماء."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Where Do I Belong? – Saad","body":"Having grown up in Taif, I always thought I knew exactly who I was.\nThen, at fourteen, I joined an international summer camp in Abha, where I met students from twelve countries.\nSurrounded by different languages and traditions, I suddenly felt unsure about my own identity.\nHowever, sharing stories around the campfire, I realized that we had more in common than I had imagined.\nOur friendship, built on tolerance and curiosity, grew stronger every day.\nNot wanting the camp to end, we created an online group to stay in touch.\nI learned that belonging does not mean being the same; it means accepting our differences.","word_count":105,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Saad's paragraph. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Saad grew up in Abha.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The camp had students from twelve countries.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"At first, Saad felt unsure about his identity.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Saad discovered that the campers had nothing in common.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The campers created an online group to stay in touch.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Saad thinks belonging means being the same as everyone.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Where Do I Belong? – Saad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ up in Taif, Saad thought he knew exactly who he was.","options":["Having been grown","Having grown","Grown","To grow"],"answer":"Having grown","explanation":"Participle clause (form or meaning).","explanation_ar":"جملة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"___ by different languages, he felt unsure about his identity.","options":["To surround","Surrounding","Surrounded","Having surrounded"],"answer":"Surrounded","explanation":"Participle clause (form or meaning).","explanation_ar":"جملة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"Their friendship, built on ___ and curiosity, grew stronger.","options":["tolerance","tolerant","tolerantly","tolerate"],"answer":"tolerance","explanation":"Abstract noun.","explanation_ar":"الاسم المجرد."},{"q":"","options":["Not wanted the camp to end, they created an online group.","Wanting not the camp to end, they created an online group.","Not wanting the camp to end, an online group was created.","Not wanting the camp to end, they created an online group."],"answer":"Not wanting the camp to end, they created an online group.","explanation":"Participle clause (form or meaning).","explanation_ar":"جملة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"","options":["Their friendship, built on tolerance grew stronger every day.","Their friendship built, on tolerance grew stronger every day.","Their friendship built on tolerance, grew stronger every day.","Their friendship, built on tolerance, grew stronger every day."],"answer":"Their friendship, built on tolerance, grew stronger every day.","explanation":"Commas and logical subjects with participial phrases.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل والفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"At first, Saad felt like an outsider among the campers. ______ after a few evenings of sharing stories, he realized how much they had in common.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","However,","Therefore,","For example,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Identity circles: write your notes.","items":[{"q":"The object you chose and what it represents: ___","options":[],"answer":"My grandmother's coffee pot – it represents my family's hospitality.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence about it with a participle clause: ___","options":[],"answer":"Passed down from my great-grandmother, the coffee pot is over eighty years old.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"An abstract noun connected to your object: ___","options":[],"answer":"generosity","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A question you asked a classmate: ___","options":[],"answer":"Having lived in Egypt, do you feel Egyptian or Saudi?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A connection you made: ___","options":[],"answer":"Having moved cities too, I understood how he felt.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your reflective paragraph.","items":[{"q":"Plan: the situation, your feelings, what changed, what you learned.","options":[],"answer":"first day at a new school in Riyadh; not knowing anyone; felt invisible; a classmate invited me to play football; friendship; belonging comes from small acts of kindness","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your paragraph in 8–9 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Having moved from Taif to Riyadh last year, I started at a school where I knew nobody. Not knowing anyone, I spent the first week eating lunch alone. Surrounded by noisy groups of friends, I felt almost invisible. Then a boy named Hamad, sitting at the next table, invited me to play football. That small act of kindness changed everything. Encouraged by his friendship, I joined the school team. Our coach, trained in Spain, helped me improve my skills. Today I feel a real sense of belonging. I learned that kindness can turn strangers into friends.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: participle clauses with the right subject? commas after introductory phrases? abstract nouns spelled correctly? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"Not knowing anyone, the first week was lonely\" to \"Not knowing anyone, I spent the first week eating lunch alone.\"","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Treasure Hunt Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams move to a station every 90 seconds.","At each station, write one correct sentence with the cue word and participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","Teams collect a \"treasure letter\" for each correct sentence.","Unscramble the letters at the end to reveal the secret word."],"data":[{"cue":"identity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"belong","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"abroad","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"unfamiliar","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"adapt","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"accent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"diversity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"tolerance","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"nationality","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"citizenship","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"solidarity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"adolescence","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"acceptance","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"roots","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"bilingual","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ the oldest son, Faisal felt responsible for his younger brothers.","options":["Been","To be","Having being","Being"],"answer":"Being","explanation":"Participle clause (form or meaning).","explanation_ar":"جملة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"The flood forced families to leave, ___ their homes empty for months.","options":["to leave","leaving","having been left","left"],"answer":"leaving","explanation":"Participle clause (form or meaning).","explanation_ar":"جملة اسم الفاعل أو المفعول."},{"q":"Many young people search for their ___ during adolescence.","options":["identical","identically","identity","identify"],"answer":"identity","explanation":"Abstract noun.","explanation_ar":"الاسم المجرد."},{"q":"The ___ of the community after the fire was amazing; everyone helped.","options":["solid","solidly","solidarity","solidify"],"answer":"solidarity","explanation":"Abstract noun.","explanation_ar":"الاسم المجرد."},{"q":"","options":["Having studied the map, the travelers chose the safest route.","Having studied the map, the route seemed safest.","Having studied the map, safety was the travelers' choice.","Having studied the map, the safest route was chosen."],"answer":"Having studied the map, the travelers chose the safest route.","explanation":"Commas and logical subjects with participial phrases.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل والفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"Moving to a new country can be lonely at first. ______ many international students say that their first friends become friends for life.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","Likewise,","For instance,","However,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Being bilingual offers many advantages. ______ bilingual children often find it easier to learn a third language.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","Nevertheless,","Instead,","However,"],"answer":"For example,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Many Saudi students study abroad on scholarships.\n• Living abroad helps them become more independent.\n• They learn about other cultures.\n• Many return home to work in key industries.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize how studying abroad benefits Saudi Arabia. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Having studied abroad, many Saudi scholarship students return home to work in key industries, bringing new skills to the country.","Living abroad helps students become independent.","Students learn about other cultures when they live abroad.","Many Saudi students study abroad on scholarships."],"answer":"Having studied abroad, many Saudi scholarship students return home to work in key industries, bringing new skills to the country.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Third culture kids are children raised in a culture different from their parents'.\n• They often move between countries.\n• Many are bilingual or multilingual.\n• Some say they feel they belong everywhere and nowhere.\n\nThe student wants to describe a challenge third culture kids may face. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Many children move between countries.","Third culture kids are raised in a different culture.","Third culture kids are often bilingual or multilingual.","Having moved between countries, some third culture kids say that they feel they belong everywhere and nowhere."],"answer":"Having moved between countries, some third culture kids say that they feel they belong everywhere and nowhere.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for a reflective paragraph:","options":["Welcoming by my new classmates, I soon felt a real sense of belonging.","Welcomed by my new classmates, I soon felt a real sense of belonging.","Welcomed by my new classmates, a real sense of belonging came to me.","Welcomed by my new classmates I soon felt a real sense of belong."],"answer":"Welcomed by my new classmates, I soon felt a real sense of belonging.","explanation":"Commas and logical subjects with participial phrases.","explanation_ar":"الفواصل والفاعل المنطقي."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"An international summer camp in Abha: teenagers from many countries around a campfire under the stars, sharing food and stories; Saad in the center listening and smiling. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An international summer camp in Abha: teenagers from many countries around a campfire under the stars, sharing food and stories; Saad in the center listening and smiling"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Identity and Belonging”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Identity and Belonging”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L09-U1-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Treasure Hunt Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Treasure Hunt Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS5-T1","script":"Having grown up in Taif, I always thought I knew exactly who I was. Then, at fourteen, I joined an international summer camp in Abha, where I met students from twelve countries. Surrounded by different languages and traditions, I suddenly felt unsure about my own identity. However, sharing stories around the campfire, I realized that we had more in common than I had imagined. Our friendship, built on tolerance and curiosity, grew stronger every day. Not wanting the camp to end, we created an online group to stay in touch. I learned that belonging does not mean being the same; it means accepting our differences.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.0"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L09-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Where Do I Belong?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn participle clauses (form and meaning), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and dangling participles; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Identity circles: in groups, each student shares one object (a photo, a word in another language, a family recipe) that represents part of his identity, using one participle clause and one abstract noun. Listeners respond with a question and a sentence of connection (\"Having moved too, I understand…\"). Today you use all of Unit 1: participle clauses (present, past, perfect; time, reason, result, condition), abstract nouns, commas with participial phrases and logical subjects. A reflective paragraph tells a story, explains feelings and states a lesson. Participle clauses make your writing more mature; abstract nouns help you talk about ideas; transitions such as however, as a result, in the end show how your thinking changed. Here are some examples. Having moved schools three times, I know how it feels to be new. Feeling nervous, I stood at the back of the room. Welcomed by my classmates, I soon felt at home. Be careful! Some learners say: “Having grown up in Taif, my identity seemed clear.” That’s not right. We say: “Having grown up in Taif, I thought my identity was clear.” Dangling participle → correct subject. Quick check: what is wrong with “Surrounded by new languages I felt unsure.”? … Think! … It should be “Surrounded by new languages, I felt unsure.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about what makes me feel I belong.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.0"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"___ in Makkah, Hamad has visited the Grand Mosque hundreds of times.","options":["To be born","Having born","Bearing","Born"],"answer":"Born","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"___ his exams, Omar went camping with his cousins.","options":["Having been finished","Having finished","To finish","Finished by"],"answer":"Having finished","explanation":"Perfect participle (having + past participle): earlier action.","explanation_ar":"Having done."},{"q":"___ by the crowd's support, the runner sprinted to the finish line.","options":["Having encouraged","Encouraging","Encouraged","To encourage"],"answer":"Encouraged","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"___ twice, the young writer finally had her first novel published.","options":["Being reject","Having been rejected","Rejecting","Having rejected"],"answer":"Having been rejected","explanation":"Perfect passive (having been + past participle).","explanation_ar":"Having been done."},{"q":"___ what to say, I stayed silent.","options":["Knowing not","Not knowing","No knowing","Not knew"],"answer":"Not knowing","explanation":"Negative: not + participle.","explanation_ar":"النفي."},{"q":"______ in a small village near Al-Ula, the photographer has spent his career capturing the beauty of the desert.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["To raise","Having raised","Raising","Raised"],"answer":"Raised","explanation":"Past participle: passive meaning.","explanation_ar":"اسم المفعول."},{"q":"___ the youngest in the team, Ziyad was nervous before the final.","options":["Having being","Been","To be","Being"],"answer":"Being","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."},{"q":"The heavy snow closed the roads, ___ thousands of travelers at the airport.","options":["to strand","having stranded them","stranding","stranded"],"answer":"stranding","explanation":"Result (…, leaving / causing / making).","explanation_ar":"النتيجة."},{"q":"___ correctly, this medicine is completely safe.","options":["Taken","Taking","To take","Having taken"],"answer":"Taken","explanation":"Condition (if).","explanation_ar":"الشرط."},{"q":"After ___ the news, she called her parents immediately.","options":["hearing","heard","having been heard","hear"],"answer":"hearing","explanation":"Conjunction or preposition + participle.","explanation_ar":"أداة الربط + الصيغة."},{"q":"Our city is proud of its cultural ___.","options":["diversely","diverse","diversify","diversity"],"answer":"diversity","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"My grandmother loves telling stories about her ___.","options":["childness","childhood","childship","childdom"],"answer":"childhood","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"The ___ of the old man came from ninety years of experience.","options":["wiseship","wiseness","wisehood","wisdom"],"answer":"wisdom","explanation":"Spelling changes with suffixes.","explanation_ar":"تغيّر التهجئة."},{"q":"After decades abroad, the poet wrote movingly about returning to his ______, the mountain village where his family had lived for generations.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["nationality","accent","roots","adolescence"],"answer":"roots","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"People who respect others' beliefs and customs show ___.","options":["tolerant","tolerantly","tolerance","tolerate"],"answer":"tolerance","explanation":"Choose the right suffix.","explanation_ar":"اللاحقة الصحيحة."},{"q":"","options":["Raised in Abha, Noura loves rainy days.","Raised in Abha Noura loves rainy days.","Raised, in Abha Noura loves rainy days.","Raised in Abha Noura, loves rainy days."],"answer":"Raised in Abha, Noura loves rainy days.","explanation":"Comma after an introductory participial phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة بعد العبارة الافتتاحية."},{"q":"","options":["The museum, opened in 2019 attracts many visitors.","The museum opened in 2019, attracts many visitors.","The museum opened, in 2019 attracts many visitors.","The museum, opened in 2019, attracts many visitors."],"answer":"The museum, opened in 2019, attracts many visitors.","explanation":"Pair of commas around a nonessential phrase.","explanation_ar":"فاصلتان حول العبارة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"","options":["The students taking the bus must arrive by 6:30.","The students taking the bus, must arrive by 6:30.","The students, taking the bus must arrive by 6:30.","The students, taking the bus, must arrive by 6:30."],"answer":"The students taking the bus must arrive by 6:30.","explanation":"Essential phrase → no commas.","explanation_ar":"العبارة الضرورية بلا فواصل."},{"q":"Having trained for the marathon for six months, ______\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the race was finished by Khalid in under four hours.","under four hours was Khalid's finishing time.","Khalid finished the race in under four hours.","Khalid's time was under four hours."],"answer":"Khalid finished the race in under four hours.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"Many people assume that bilingual children get confused between their two languages. ______ research shows that most bilingual children separate their languages easily.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","In fact,","Therefore,","Similarly,"],"answer":"In fact,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Hospitality is a central value in Arab culture.\n• Guests are traditionally offered Arabic coffee and dates.\n• The coffee is served in small cups.\n• Serving coffee is a sign of respect.\n\nThe student wants to explain the meaning behind serving Arabic coffee. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Offered to guests as a sign of respect, Arabic coffee reflects the central value of hospitality in Arab culture.","Guests are offered coffee and dates.","Arab culture has many traditions.","Arabic coffee is served in small cups."],"answer":"Offered to guests as a sign of respect, Arabic coffee reflects the central value of hospitality in Arab culture.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"In which sentence does the opening phrase describe the wrong subject?","options":["Opening the door, a cold wind blew into the room.","When I opened the door, a cold wind blew into the room.","A cold wind blew into the room when I opened the door.","Opening the door, I felt a cold wind blow into the room."],"answer":"Opening the door, a cold wind blew into the room.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"having lived in japan, sushi is his favorite food.\"","options":["Having lived in Japan sushi is his favorite food.","Having lived in Japan, sushi is his favorite food.","Having lived in Japan, he loves sushi more than any other food.","having lived in Japan, he loves sushi more than any other food."],"answer":"Having lived in Japan, he loves sushi more than any other food.","explanation":"Choose the logical subject after the phrase.","explanation_ar":"الفاعل المنطقي."},{"q":"\"Feeling homesick, he called his mother.\" What does the participle clause show?","options":["a condition","the reason he called","the place he called from","the result of the call"],"answer":"the reason he called","explanation":"Reason (because / as).","explanation_ar":"السبب."}],"theme_ar":"الهوية والانتماء","essential_question_ar":"ما الذي يشكّل هويتنا ومكان انتمائنا؟","can_do":"I can use present, past and perfect participle clauses to express time, reason, result and condition, form abstract nouns with suffixes, and punctuate participial phrases correctly without dangling modifiers.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أستخدم جمل اسم الفاعل والمفعول والصيغ التامة للتعبير عن الزمن والسبب والنتيجة والشرط، وأكوّن الأسماء المجردة باللواحق، وأرقّم العبارات الوصفية بشكل صحيح دون مُعدِّلات معلّقة."});
